## Goal
Run a principal component analysis of the gene count matrix and plot the samples with points
coloured by condition, to show how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) separate
in expression space.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | `01_prepare_samplesheets` (native) | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1), submitted
through the executor door. Inputs are read only; nothing is written outside `results/`.

1. Resolve both inputs with `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk
   --consumes counts_gene design`; stop with an error if either is missing.
2. Read the count matrix (tab-separated; `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample).
   Drop `gene_name`; index by `gene_id`. Read the design table and take `sample_id` and
   `condition`. Fail if the sample columns of the matrix and the design's `sample_id` values are
   not the same set; order the matrix columns to match the design.
3. Drop genes with zero counts in every sample.
4. Normalise for library size and stabilise variance: counts per million
   (count / sample column total x 1e6), then `log2(CPM + 1)`.
5. Feature selection: keep the 500 genes with the highest variance of log2(CPM + 1) across
   samples, or all retained genes if fewer than 500 remain (the matrix holds 200 genes, so in
   practice all of them are used).
6. Centre each gene to mean 0 across samples (no scaling to unit variance), then fit
   `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` with `n_components = n_samples - 1` (5) and
   `svd_solver="full"`.
7. Write the per-sample PC scores with condition, and the explained-variance ratio per
   component.
8. Plot PC1 against PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by `condition` with two
   distinct categorical colours and a legend, each point labelled with its sample ID, axis
   labels showing the percentage of variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Save as PNG at
   300 dpi.

